WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:01.539
Erik Oleson: &nbsp; Hi, I’m Erik Oleson.&nbsp;

00:00:01.539 --> 00:00:02.539
I’m here from THE MAN IN THE HIGH CASTLE.

00:00:02.539 --> 00:00:06.939
Film Courage:&nbsp; Erik, how many years did it
take to get this television show made and

00:00:06.939 --> 00:00:07.960
how did it finally happen?

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:13.730
Erik Oleson:&nbsp; I was hired to join the show
for season two.&nbsp;

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:23.009
Well, I know from the novel which was written
decades ago [author Philip K. Dick], the original

00:00:23.009 --> 00:00:29.020
producers of the series struggled for many,
many years to bring it to the screen.&nbsp;

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:32.970
It was a long development process, over a
decade.&nbsp;

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:36.090
So I was fortunate enough to be brought on
in season two.

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:38.980
&nbsp; Film Courage:&nbsp; Had you already watched
the show?

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:40.460
Erik Oleson:&nbsp; Oh yes!&nbsp;

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:42.160
I’d watched all of season one.&nbsp;

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:49.120
And I came on board and ended up being the
head writer of season two.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:51.980
Film Courage:&nbsp; Do you remember when you sat
down to write for them?

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:55.750
I don’t know if you prepared a spec script
for them or what the process was?&nbsp;

00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:59.800
What was the first presentation of your work
to the show’s developers?

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:04.781
Erik Oleson: &nbsp; Well, I had written on 15
other television shows series prior to THE

00:01:04.781 --> 00:01:07.141
MAN IN THE HIGH CASTLE, so my work was kind
of known.&nbsp;

00:01:07.141 --> 00:01:12.180
I’m also an executive producer so they knew
I could handle a lot of the other aspects

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:14.610
that go along with writing a big television
show like this.&nbsp;

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:20.950
But the thing that I’d written that probably
put me most in contention, I’d written a

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:26.690
pilot about Nazi occupied Paris, which was
a historically based pilot.&nbsp;

00:01:26.690 --> 00:01:30.880
So I think a lot of the folks read that and
said “He knows how to do a Nazi show.”

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:34.130
&nbsp; Film Courage:&nbsp; What are your first steps
for writing a screenplay?&nbsp;

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:39.020
Do you have a set of tried-and-true set of
rules that you use?&nbsp;

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:41.110
Or is every script different?

00:01:41.110 --> 00:01:46.180
Erik Oleson:&nbsp; My process evolves every time
I do another job.&nbsp;

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:49.409
I realize how much I didn’t know in my prior
job.&nbsp;

00:01:49.409 --> 00:01:59.159
So I’m someone who reads pretty much every
trick, every screenwriting book, every script

00:01:59.159 --> 00:02:04.790
that I can get my hands on and I’m constantly
trying to evolve as a craftsman.&nbsp;

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:10.769
I am also fortunate enough in this job to
surround myself with really talented people

00:02:10.769 --> 00:02:14.209
and you learn a lot from other writers.&nbsp;

00:02:14.209 --> 00:02:20.560
There were writers on THE MAN IN THE HIGH
CASTLE that won Emmys, or Martin Scorsese’s

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:26.739
best friend…you really want to kind of learn
from the people around you and that makes

00:02:26.739 --> 00:02:27.739
you better.&nbsp;

00:02:27.739 --> 00:02:35.049
So I can’t say that I have any set process,
although right now I have to say I’m a big

00:02:35.049 --> 00:02:39.579
fan of the John Truby stuff.&nbsp;

00:02:39.579 --> 00:02:42.719
I think he kind of hits the sweet spot for
me.&nbsp;

00:02:42.719 --> 00:02:45.049
But I’ve taken them all.&nbsp;

00:02:45.049 --> 00:02:46.809
I’ve taken [Robert] McKee.&nbsp;

00:02:46.809 --> 00:02:53.260
I’m one of these guys who likes to learn
new stuff about the craft and think if I can

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:59.389
learn something from any book, if I can learn
one thing from it and it makes me a better

00:02:59.389 --> 00:03:01.739
writer, I’m all for it.

00:03:01.739 --> 00:03:06.060
Film Courage:&nbsp; Have you ever written a screenplay
that has no structure?

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:12.439
Erik Oleson:&nbsp; I am more of a planner than
a “pantser.”&nbsp;

00:03:12.439 --> 00:03:17.279
Obviously like Stephen King will say he swears
by the seat-of-your-pants approach.&nbsp;

00:03:17.279 --> 00:03:25.029
But I’m much more of an outliner, an engineer
when it comes to that.&nbsp;

00:03:25.029 --> 00:03:31.140
And you throw that out and you let the character
speak to you and gather the moment and things

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:32.219
change.&nbsp;

00:03:32.219 --> 00:03:35.959
But I’m also a big fan of structure I have
to say.&nbsp;

00:03:35.959 --> 00:03:44.499
So no, I haven’t sat down and tried to write
a final product from page one.&nbsp;

00:03:44.499 --> 00:03:47.689
I kind of construct it over time.&nbsp;

00:03:47.689 --> 00:03:49.599
I’m a grinder I guess you could say.

00:03:49.599 --> 00:03:53.859
Film Courage:&nbsp; When you begin to write, are
you envisioning the people watching the final

00:03:53.859 --> 00:03:56.799
product or does that not even enter into your
mind?&nbsp;

00:03:56.799 --> 00:04:02.260
You’re so involved with the beginning and
what’s in your own head?

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:07.689
Erik Oleson:&nbsp; You want to keep the audience
in mind somewhat but you also don’t want

00:04:07.689 --> 00:04:08.689
to pander.&nbsp;

00:04:08.689 --> 00:04:13.859
Like, if you’re in the moment, much as an
actor is in the moment, you’re trying to

00:04:13.859 --> 00:04:17.730
find the humanity and the truth behind what
you’re writing about.&nbsp;

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:24.040
And whether you’re doing a superhero show,
whether you’re doing a big, deep, socio-political

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:31.040
show like THE MAN IN THE HIGH CASTLE you actually
find yourself lost in the moment and how the

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:38.160
characters are speaking to each other as real
people and that’s the joy of discovery that

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:45.410
happens when you have enough (for me) of the
structure figured out that you have the correct

00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:47.100
characters in the scene.

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:52.090
You’re in the moment and they start to talk
to you when it works.

00:04:52.090 --> 00:04:56.720
Film Courage:&nbsp; Well speaking of talking,
this will be my last question but with dialogue,

00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:01.100
how much are you refining the dialogue and
making sure that you’re researching it for

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:03.890
that specific time period and trying it out
on other people?

00:05:03.890 --> 00:05:05.140
Erik Oleson:&nbsp; Constantly.&nbsp;

00:05:05.140 --> 00:05:09.930
There is a constant polish process that goes
on for dialogue.&nbsp;

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:17.410
Also, we had researchers who were making sure
that we didn’t use words that didn’t exist

00:05:17.410 --> 00:05:26.720
and sometimes they would catch us making an
anachronistic, modern kind of “Oh yeah.&nbsp;

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:27.720
That doesn’t work.&nbsp;

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:29.490
We have to change the line.”&nbsp;

00:05:29.490 --> 00:05:32.980
Because we all are creatures of pop culture,
as well.&nbsp;

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:38.780
In THE MAN IN THE HIGH CASTLE, which takes
place in the early 1960’s, I had to avoid

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:41.630
references from some of my favorite superheroes.

00:05:41.630 --> 00:05:43.220
Film Courage:&nbsp; Okay.&nbsp;

00:05:43.220 --> 00:05:47.170
Any other terminology, I mean definitely not
using the word “Bro.”&nbsp;

00:05:47.170 --> 00:05:49.190
That’s become part of the culture.&nbsp;

00:05:49.190 --> 00:05:54.610
Were there certain things in the 1960’s
that we don’t even realize it wasn’t part

00:05:54.610 --> 00:05:55.610
of the vernacular?

00:05:55.610 --> 00:05:57.490
Erik Oleson:&nbsp; Correct.&nbsp;

00:05:57.490 --> 00:06:01.910
In the case of THE MAN IN THE HIGH CASTLE
it’s an alternative world also where history

00:06:01.910 --> 00:06:07.560
has turned out differently from the tale of
World War II, so a lot of the language that

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:13.970
we would use in a first draft kind of would
be slightly different like given the way the

00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:17.840
world turned out and cultural experience.&nbsp;

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:24.330
And in THE MAN IN THE HIGH CASTLE, white people
are not the overclass in the Pacific States.&nbsp;

00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:33.220
They are kind of treated as an oppressed minority
and so there are all sorts of textures that

00:06:33.220 --> 00:06:37.410
seed into the language when you have that
kind of story.&nbsp;

00:06:37.410 --> 00:06:42.890
So it’s an alt-history storyline and language
was very important to us and we had a lot

00:06:42.890 --> 00:06:47.490
of very smart people making sure that we were
polishing it up right to the moment the cameras

00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:48.490
rolled.

00:06:48.490 --> 00:06:49.490
Film Courage:&nbsp; Excellent!&nbsp;

00:06:49.490 --> 00:06:50.490
Thank you Erik.

00:06:50.490 --> 00:06:50.494
Erik Oleson:&nbsp; Thank you!

